package clwd:identity@0.2.0;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// DECISION 1 — ambient `get-caller()` vs a threaded request-context resource.
//
// Chosen: AMBIENT.
//
// Rationale: app code here is largely written by agents. A threaded context
// resource puts an extra parameter on every handler and every helper the
// handler calls; agents drop it, or worse, stash it in a global to avoid
// threading it — which reintroduces exactly the bug the resource was meant
// to prevent, only harder to see.
//
// The cost is a real invariant, and it is NOT free. `wasi:http/proxy`, which
// world `clwd:app` includes, explicitly permits the opposite:
//
// "A host may arbitrarily reuse or not reuse component instance when
// delivering incoming HTTP requests and thus a component must be able to
// handle 0..N calls to `handle`."
//
// Ambient identity is only sound if clwd overrides that permission. So:
//
//   INVARIANT (binding on clwd-runtime, both backends):
//   A component instance observes exactly one caller for its whole lifetime.
//   An instance that has handled a request for caller A is never given a
//   request for caller B. Backends satisfy this by not reusing instances
//   across callers at all, or by tearing down and re-establishing ambient
//   state between callers such that no value from A is readable by B.
//
//   For `wasm`: one instance per request, or pooled strictly per-caller.
//   For `microvm`: the VM is long-lived by design, so the guest agent must
//   scope identity per request rather than per instance. This is the weaker
//   backend for this property, and per SECURITY.md a gap here is a
//   vulnerability in that backend, not a documentation gap.
//
// If that invariant ever becomes unenforceable, this decision must be
// revisited by adding a request-context resource — not by relaxing the
// invariant and leaving `get-caller()` in place.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Identity of the caller for the current request.
///
/// Every value here is populated by the gateway before app code runs.
/// There is deliberately no way for a component to construct, mutate, or
/// re-authenticate a principal — if an app could, an authoring agent would
/// eventually wire up its own login flow and defeat the platform.
interface types {
    /// Opaque, stable, org-unique. Not an email: emails get renamed and reused.
    type subject-id = string;
    type org-id = string;

    record principal {
        subject: subject-id,
        org: org-id,
        display-name: option<string>,
        email: option<string>,
    }

    /// A non-human caller: another app, or an agent holding a delegated grant.
    record service-principal {
        subject: subject-id,
        org: org-id,
        on-behalf-of: option<subject-id>,
    }

    /// Anonymous is reachable — a public share link has no session.
    /// Apps must handle it rather than assuming a user is present.
    variant caller {
        anonymous,
        user(principal),
        service(service-principal),
    }

    /// Maps 1:1 onto a Cedar entity UID (`Type::"id"`).
    record entity-ref {
        kind: string,
        id: string,
    }

    /// One question for `authorize-many`. Field-for-field identical to the
    /// arguments of `authorize`, so the single and batch forms cannot drift.
    record authz-request {
        action: string,
        target: entity-ref,
    }

    record decision {
        allowed: bool,
        /// Diagnostic only. Never branch on this string.
        reason: option<string>,
    }

    variant authz-error {
        /// Control plane unreachable. Callers must fail closed.
        unavailable,
        malformed(string),
    }
}

interface context {
    use types.{caller, entity-ref, authz-request, decision, authz-error};

    /// Who is calling, for the request currently being handled.
    ///
    /// Ambient by design — see DECISION 1 at the top of this file, and the
    /// instance-reuse invariant it places on clwd-runtime.
    get-caller: func() -> caller;

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // DECISION 3 (0.2.0) — no roles and no groups until something fills them.
    //
    // 0.1.0 had `get-role`, `in-group` and `list-groups`. No host populates
    // roles or groups yet, so every caller had no role and no groups, and an
    // app's check failed OPEN: `!in-group("banned")` is true for everyone, and
    // `role != viewer` holds for a caller with no role at all. They are
    // removed rather than kept empty, because an always-empty answer is one an
    // app author will build authorization on.
    //
    // They come back, additively, in a minor version once a host can answer
    // them truthfully. Until then the authorization question goes through
    // `authorize`, which fails closed.
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------

    /// Delegate an authorization question to Cedar in the control plane,
    /// for resources the app owns and the platform can't see.
    authorize: func(
        action: string,
        target: entity-ref,
    ) -> result<decision, authz-error>;

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // DECISION 2 — does `authorize` need batching now?
    //
    // Chosen: YES, ship `authorize-many` (since 0.1.0).
    //
    // The motivating case is ordinary, not exotic: a list view over 500 rows
    // filtered by permission. With only the scalar form that is 500 host
    // round-trips per page render. On `wasm` those are in-process and merely
    // wasteful; on `microvm` each one is a vsock round-trip, which turns a
    // list view into a latency outlier and puts a per-request cost on the
    // host that scales with the caller's data, not with the request.
    //
    // Adding this later is not a additive change in practice — by then
    // templates and generated apps have standardized on the N-call shape,
    // and the fix requires rewriting app code we do not own. The contract is
    // cheap to widen now and expensive to widen once templates exist.
    //
    // Deliberately NOT solved here: a predicate/filter form ("give me the
    // subset of these 500 the caller may read"). That needs Cedar partial
    // evaluation to be worth having, and guessing its shape now would be the
    // speculative version of this same mistake.
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------

    /// Batch form of `authorize`. Results are positional: index i in the
    /// returned list is the decision for index i of `requests`.
    ///
    /// The error case is whole-batch. A batch either yields a decision for
    /// every request or fails outright — there is no partial-success shape,
    /// because a caller that got 400 of 500 answers has no safe default for
    /// the other 100 and would have to fail closed anyway.
    authorize-many: func(
        requests: list<authz-request>,
    ) -> result<list<decision>, authz-error>;
}
